                <pb facs="0541" n="397" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg397"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/597: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Perkins</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-12-06T11:00:00+08:00">December 6, 1929—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 6—3:12 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1095. My 1084 [<hi rendition="#italic">1093</hi>], December 5, 7 p.m. Following
                    from American Consul in charge at Mukden: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“December 5, 10 p.m. My telegram of December 5, 11 a.m. Reliably informed
                            that discussions concerning Emshanoff, Eismont were continued today. The
                            Marshal eventually acquiesced in their appointment to other positions. A
                            reply to Litvinoff’s telegram of November 27th<anchor n="74"
                                xml:id="d343-f1"/> in this sense was sent today. Apparently no
                            message was sent last night. Lu Jung-huan has not signed [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">sic</hi>]; Mukden has now agreed to the
                            conditions of Soviet for starting negotiations.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d343-f1" n="74" xml:id="d343-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 1054, November 29, from the Chargé in China, <ref
                        target="#pg362">p. 362</ref>.</p>
            </note>
